      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we start by using the trigonometric identity for the cosine of a sum:\n\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B = \\cos (A + B)\n\\]\n\nIn our equation, let \\(A = 2x\\) and \\(B = 3x\\). Then we have:\n\n\\[\n\\cos (2x + 3x) - \\sin (2x + 3x) = 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 5x - \\sin 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = \\sin 5x\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\sin \\theta = \\cos \\theta\\) when \\(\\theta = 45^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\\) for any integer \\(k\\). Therefore, we can set:\n\n\\[\n5x = 45^\\circ + 180^\\circ k\n\\]\n\nTo find the smallest positive angle \\(x\\), we set \\(k = 0\\):\n\n\\[\n5x = 45^\\circ \\implies x = \\frac{45^\\circ}{5} = 9^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
