      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we start by using a trigonometric identity that relates sine and cosine products. The identity we need is:\n\n\\[\n\\sin A \\sin B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) - \\cos (A + B)]\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos A \\cos B = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (A - B) + \\cos (A + B)]\n\\]\n\nApplying this identity to our equation, we set \\(A = 2x\\) and \\(B = 3x\\). Then,\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x - 3x) - \\cos (2x + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-x) - \\cos (5x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x]\n\\]\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (2x - 3x) + \\cos (2x + 3x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos (-x) + \\cos (5x)] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x + \\cos 5x]\n\\]\n\nThus, the original equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x - \\cos 5x] = \\frac{1}{2} [\\cos x + \\cos 5x]\n\\]\n\nMultiplying both sides by 2, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos x - \\cos 5x = \\cos x + \\cos 5x\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(\\cos x\\) from both sides, we obtain:\n\n\\[\n- \\cos 5x = \\cos 5x\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n2 \\cos 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n\\cos 5x = 0\n\\]\n\nThe cosine function is zero at odd multiples of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n5x = 90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k \\quad \\text{for integer } k\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{90^\\circ + 180^\\circ k}{5} = 18^\\circ + 36^\\circ k\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive angle \\(x\\). For \\(k = 0\\),\n\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}\n\\]",
