      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the equation \\(\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x\\), we start by using the trigonometric identity for the cosine of a sum:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(A + B) = \\cos A \\cos B - \\sin A \\sin B.\n\\]\n\nRewriting the given equation using this identity, we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin 2x \\sin 3x = \\cos 2x \\cos 3x.\n\\]\n\nThis can be rearranged to:\n\n\\[\n\\cos 2x \\cos 3x - \\sin 2x \\sin 3x = 0.\n\\]\n\nUsing the identity for the cosine of a sum, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos(2x + 3x) = \\cos 5x = 0.\n\\]\n\nThe cosine function is zero at odd multiples of \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) radians. Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\n5x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi,\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(k\\) is an integer. Solving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{\\pi}{10} + \\frac{k\\pi}{5}.\n\\]\n\nTo express \\(x\\) in degrees, we convert \\(\\pi\\) radians to degrees by multiplying by \\(\\frac{180}{\\pi}\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{180}{10} + \\frac{180k}{5} = 18 + 36k.\n\\]\n\nThe smallest positive angle \\(x\\) occurs when \\(k = 0\\):\n\n\\[\nx = 18^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nThus, the smallest positive angle \\(x\\) that satisfies the given equation is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{18}.\n\\]",
